       The ttmmppnnaamm() function returns a pointer to a string  that  is  a  valid
       filename,  and  such  that  a file with this name did not exist at some
       point in time, so that naive programmers may think it a  suitable  name
       for  a  temporary file.  If the argument _s is NULL, this name is gener‐
       ated in an internal static buffer and may be overwritten  by  the  next
       call  to ttmmppnnaamm().  If _s is not NULL, the name is copied to the charac‐
       ter array (of length at least _L___t_m_p_n_a_m) pointed to by _s and the value _s
       is returned in case of success.

       The created pathname has a directory prefix _P___t_m_p_d_i_r.   (Both  _L___t_m_p_n_a_m
       and  _P___t_m_p_d_i_r are defined in _<_s_t_d_i_o_._h_>, just like the TTMMPP__MMAAXX mentioned
       below.)

       The ttmmppnnaamm__rr() function performs the same task as ttmmppnnaamm(), but returns
       NULL (to indicate an error) if _s is NULL.

