NNOOTTEESS
       The ttmmppnnaamm() function generates a different  string  each  time  it  is
       called,  up to TTMMPP__MMAAXX times.  If it is called more than TTMMPP__MMAAXX times,
       the behavior is implementation defined.

       Although these functions generate names that are difficult to guess, it
       is nevertheless possible that between the time  that  the  pathname  is
       returned  and the time that the program opens it, another program might
       create that pathname using ooppeenn(2), or create it as  a  symbolic  link.
       This  can lead to security holes.  To avoid such possibilities, use the
       ooppeenn(2)  OO__EEXXCCLL  flag  to  open  the  pathname.   Or  better  yet,  use
       mmkksstteemmpp(3) or ttmmppffiillee(3).

       Portable applications that use threads cannot call ttmmppnnaamm() with a NULL
